Your understanding is wrong. Kafka has no per-record ack: each poll() moves the partition's position past every record it returns, and your commitSync() then commits those positions, failed records included. The group never sees them again; that is how the 2,300 labels went missing while lag fell to 0.

Per partition, stop at the first failure, rewind and back off:

while (running) {
  ConsumerRecords<String, LabelRequest> recs = consumer.poll(Duration.ofMillis(500));
  Map<TopicPartition, OffsetAndMetadata> commit = new HashMap<>();
  for (TopicPartition tp : recs.partitions()) {
    for (ConsumerRecord<String, LabelRequest> r : recs.records(tp)) {
      try {
        carrier.createLabel(r.value());
        commit.put(tp, new OffsetAndMetadata(r.offset() + 1));
      } catch (CarrierException e) {
        consumer.seek(tp, r.offset());          // next poll returns it again
        consumer.pause(List.of(tp));
        retryAt.put(tp, now() + backoff.next(tp));
        break;                                   // skip the rest of this partition
      }
    }
  }
  resumeDue(consumer, retryAt);                  // consumer.resume(...) when the backoff expired
  if (!commit.isEmpty()) consumer.commitSync(commit);
}

Only offsets of records that succeeded are committed, one past the last success, never past a failure. Paused partitions return nothing while poll() keeps the member alive and the other partitions flowing.

The lost labels: your warnings list topic-partition@offset. The records are still within the 7-day retention, so a small one-off consumer with assign() and seek() to each logged offset can call createLabel again. It is idempotent per requestId, so repeats are harmless.

Your code lost them, not the brokers. send() is asynchronous: it puts the record in the producer's buffer and returns. Retries do default to Integer.MAX_VALUE, but delivery.timeout.ms (default 120000 ms) bounds them. During the 3-minute leader outage the batches for orders-eu-5 and -11 expired after about 120 s with the TimeoutException you logged. Your callback only logged it, and commitSync() had already committed those orders' input offsets. So 412 orders were dropped and the lag still showed 0.

Commit only what has been delivered:

List<Future<RecordMetadata>> sent = new ArrayList<>();
for (ConsumerRecord<String, Order> r : consumer.poll(Duration.ofMillis(200)))
    sent.add(producer.send(new ProducerRecord<>(target(r), r.key(), r.value())));
producer.flush();                       // all sends of the batch completed
boolean ok = true;
for (Future<RecordMetadata> f : sent) {
    try { f.get(); } catch (ExecutionException e) { ok = false; log.error("send failed", e); }
}
if (ok) consumer.commitSync();
else rewindToCommitted(consumer);      // seek each partition to consumer.committed(...) and redo the batch

Also raise delivery.timeout.ms above what a broker roll can take, for example 300000, keeping it at least request.timeout.ms + linger.ms. That makes expiry rarer; it does not replace the check. Redoing a batch can write some orders twice: idempotence covers only the producer's own retries, so orders-eu consumers must tolerate duplicates.

Don't merge it as written. consumer.position(tp) is the offset of the next record to be fetched. That is past everything poll() has returned for tp, including the records still in your buffer. The listener commits that and then throws the buffer away, so each rebalance or shutdown would lose up to 10 minutes or 50,000 records per moved partition. The javadoc example is only right when nothing is held back.

Upload first, then commit what was uploaded:

public void onPartitionsRevoked(Collection<TopicPartition> parts) {
    Map<TopicPartition, OffsetAndMetadata> offs = new HashMap<>();
    for (TopicPartition tp : parts) {
        Buffer b = buffers.remove(tp);
        if (b != null && !b.isEmpty()) {
            upload(tp, b);                                   // synchronous
            offs.put(tp, new OffsetAndMetadata(b.lastOffset() + 1));
        }
    }
    if (!offs.isEmpty()) consumer.commitSync(offs);
}

public void onPartitionsLost(Collection<TopicPartition> parts) {
    buffers.keySet().removeAll(parts);   // may already be owned elsewhere: do not commit
}

onPartitionsLost has to be separate: by default it calls onPartitionsRevoked, and lost partitions may already have a new owner.

The revoke callback also runs on close(), so a clean SIGTERM shutdown flushes too. Leave time for the upload in the shutdown and the grace period. The callback runs inside poll() and holds up the rebalance while it uploads; that is the price of no duplicates.

Don't merge the PR. A TimeoutException from commitTransaction() means only that the producer got no answer within max.block.ms. It does not mean the commit failed: the EndTxn may already have reached the coordinator. If it did, the batch's settlements and offsets are committed, and the PR's rewind to the in-memory batch start would commit them a second time. The ledger would book duplicates. The javadoc's rule: after a commit timeout, retrying the commit is safe; any other operation is refused. That is the IllegalStateException you saw from abortTransaction().

try {
    producer.sendOffsetsToTransaction(nextOffsets, consumer.groupMetadata());
    commitWithRetry(producer);           // retries commitTransaction() on TimeoutException/InterruptException
} catch (ProducerFencedException | OutOfOrderSequenceException | AuthorizationException e) {
    producer.close(); throw e;           // fatal: restart
} catch (KafkaException e) {             // abortable send error: commit did not start
    producer.abortTransaction();
    rewindToCommitted(consumer);
}

void commitWithRetry(KafkaProducer<?, ?> p) {
    for (int i = 1; ; i++) {
        try { p.commitTransaction(); return; }
        catch (TimeoutException | InterruptException e) { if (i == 5) throw new GiveUp(e); }
    }
}

On GiveUp, close the producer and create a new one. Its initTransactions() waits for a transaction that had begun completing, or aborts one still open. Then seek to consumer.committed(assignment), which now reflects the real outcome; never use batchStart.

Reject the PR. send() does not hand the record to Kafka; it puts it in the producer's buffer and returns. The record is stored only when its callback runs without an exception. close(Duration.ZERO) does not wait: it fails every unsent and unacknowledged record at once. Each pod would therefore drop the clicks of its last linger window and in-flight requests on every rollout, after telling the clients 202.

The hang came from close() with no argument, which waits without limit. Use a bounded wait that fits in the grace period. The kubelet sends SIGTERM, then KILL when the 30 s are up.

Runtime.getRuntime().addShutdownHook(new Thread(() -> {
    http.stop(5);                              // stop accepting, let running handlers finish
    try {
        producer.close(Duration.ofSeconds(20)); // sends what is buffered, waits at most 20 s
    } catch (Exception e) {
        log.error("producer close failed", e);
    }
}));

If a broker is slow, close(20 s) still returns in time. Records it could not send then fail through their callbacks, which you log, instead of vanishing silently. If 20 s is often not enough, raise terminationGracePeriodSeconds rather than lowering the close timeout.

The reviewer is right. Idempotence lets the broker discard duplicates of the producer's own internal retries, using the producer id and sequence number. Your loop calls send() again, which creates a new record with a new sequence number, and the broker writes it too. Worse, get(10, SECONDS) timing out does not cancel the first send. During the failover it was still being retried inside the producer and succeeded later, next to your second copy. Those are the three double-counted postings.

Send once and let the producer retry:

try {
    producer.send(new ProducerRecord<>("ledger-postings", p.accountId(), p)).get();   // no shorter app timeout
} catch (ExecutionException e) {
    // failed after the producer's own retries (delivery.timeout.ms) or a non-retriable error:
    // do not resend blindly; stop, check what was last written, then decide
    throw new PostingFailed(p, e);
}

Retries already default to Integer.MAX_VALUE, bounded by delivery.timeout.ms (120 s). Raise delivery.timeout.ms if your failovers take longer.

Idempotence also covers only one producer session. A posting re-sent after a restart gets a new producer id and is not deduplicated. If postings can be re-sent across restarts, use a transactional producer, or give each posting an id and have the consumers skip ids they have already applied.

Here skipping is right, but skip exactly one record and keep a copy first. Retrying cannot help. The record at invoices-7@48213377 can never be deserialized, the position never moves past it, and every poll() throws again. That is why the partition has been stuck since 03:14.

First check that it really is one record. The error must name the same partition and offset every time, and the records after it must decode:

kafka-console-consumer.sh --bootstrap-server $BOOTSTRAP --topic invoices --partition 7 --offset 48213378 --max-messages 5 --formatter-property print.offset=true

If they do not decode either, the bug is in our deserializer. Then skipping would drop every invoice, so stop and fix the deserializer instead.

Then quarantine and skip:

} catch (RecordDeserializationException e) {
    quarantine.send(new ProducerRecord<>("invoices-quarantine", null, e.timestamp(),
            bytes(e.keyBuffer()), bytes(e.valueBuffer()), e.headers())).get();   // wait for it
    log.error("quarantined {}@{}", e.topicPartition(), e.offset(), e);
    consumer.seek(e.topicPartition(), e.offset() + 1);
}

Keep a limit: if several records in a row fail, fail the service instead of skipping them all.

What it costs: once the next commit passes 48213377, the group never reads that offset again unless someone seeks to it. The topic keeps it only for the 7 days of retention. The quarantine copy is the one finance uses.

Here dropping the backlog is the right call, done for eta-notifier only. A position older than 2 minutes has no value for this group except a wrong push. The Kafka consumer docs name this case: a time-sensitive consumer that has fallen far behind may skip to the most recent records. trip-billing is safe: positions and committed offsets are per group, and seeking in eta-notifier deletes nothing. Do not delete records or lower retention, because that would take the data away from trip-billing.

Check first that nothing else in the eta-notifier group uses these records. Then skip by staleness, not unconditionally:

ConsumerRecords<String, Position> recs = consumer.poll(Duration.ofMillis(500));
long cutoff = System.currentTimeMillis() - Duration.ofMinutes(2).toMillis();
Map<TopicPartition, Long> stale = new HashMap<>();
for (TopicPartition tp : recs.partitions()) {
    List<ConsumerRecord<String, Position>> rs = recs.records(tp);
    if (rs.get(rs.size() - 1).timestamp() < cutoff) stale.put(tp, cutoff);
}
if (!stale.isEmpty()) {
    consumer.offsetsForTimes(stale).forEach((tp, ot) -> {
        if (ot != null) consumer.seek(tp, ot.offset()); else consumer.seekToEnd(List.of(tp));
    });
}
// process only records newer than cutoff, then commitSync() as today

The new position is committed with the next commitSync(). Don't put seekToEnd() into onPartitionsAssigned: it runs on every rebalance and would drop fresh records at each deploy.
